  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Get Brexit done outside the single market, customs union and ECJ with the UK controlling laws, money, borders and trade; oppose second referendums and strengthen the Union; 20,000 more police, more stop-and-search, tougher sentencing including whole-life terms, 10,000 extra prison places; Australian-style points-based immigration system to reduce low-skilled migration with a five-year wait for benefits and higher NHS surcharge; voter ID, keep First Past the Post, and review courts/Human Rights Act/judicial review. TEXT_F: End the hostile environment, scrap the 2014 Immigration Act, end indefinite detention, close Yarl’s Wood/Brook House, and grant automatic right to remain for EU citizens; a final-say referendum with close single market alignment or remain; a public health approach to crime and a presumption against short sentences; stronger disability, race and LGBT+ rights; a Green Industrial Revolution framed around tackling the climate emergency.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_E argues from TAN commitments—national sovereignty over supranational authority, restrictive immigration, and punitive law-and-order—while TEXT_F argues from GAL commitments—expanded migrant/minority rights, less punitive justice, and pro-EU alignment on rights and standards. Given the electorate’s TAN-leaning profile (0.60 TAN vs 0.40 GAL), TEXT_E aligns more closely.",
